Keenan99说，我知道在某些电视剧中，品牌经常会被打马赛克、遮挡或重新设计。

00:15:46.251 --> 00:15:49.017
This seems to happen occasionally on Hemium.
这在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中似乎也偶尔发生过。

00:15:49.017 --> 00:15:53.794
I noticed in this episode, the shot of the TV during the Yosemite Sandy bit.
我注意到这一集中，优胜美地·桑迪那个桥段里出现的电视画面。

00:15:53.794 --> 00:15:56.120
There's a VHS tape of mad TV showing.
露出了一个《疯电视》的录像带。

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.911
Is this a planned homage to mad TV or coincidence?
这是对《疯电视》的有计划的致敬，还是巧合？

00:15:58.911 --> 00:16:00.084
That's a coincidence.
那是巧合。

00:16:00.084 --> 00:16:01.871
I don't know why that was there.
我不知道那东西为什么会出现在那儿。

00:16:01.871 --> 00:16:20.520
But I do want to say something about that bit, which is that I think that that bit is from because Chris Harris wrote the episode and that actual idea of putting a little mustache or whatever on the TV screen was I think from a story from his wife, Chris's wife, Hillary, She's also our friend and a very funny person or a fun writer.
但我确实想对那个桥段说点什么，那个桥段之所以存在，是因为克里斯·哈里斯写了这一集，而在电视屏幕上加一个小胡子之类的创意，我想是来自于他妻子克里斯的妻子希拉里的一个故事，她也是我们的朋友，是一个非常有趣的人，也是个很有意思的编剧。

00:16:20.519 --> 00:16:25.480
And she, her friend group or some friend group in college that I think may have included Paul Giamatti.
她，她的朋友圈或者大学里的某个朋友圈，我想其中可能包括保罗·吉亚玛提。

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:27.879
She was in the same area as Paul Giamatti and Yell.
她和保罗·吉亚玛提在耶鲁时就在同一个圈子里。

00:16:27.879 --> 00:16:32.639
I think it may have been part of his friend group would do this, would put a mustache on the TV.
我想那可能是他朋友圈的一部分，他们会这么做，在电视上贴一小胡子。

00:16:32.639 --> 00:16:38.320
And when it would like line up perfectly with someone, there'd be like cheers, maybe it was a drinking game, but it was a real thing.
当它和某人的脸完美契合时，大家就会欢呼，也许这是个喝酒游戏，但确有其事。

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:40.360
And Chris talked about in the room, we said, they're going to do that.
克里斯在编剧室里谈到过这件事，我们当时说，他们要这么干。

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:41.919
They're going to do that to Sandy Rivers.
他们要对桑迪·里弗斯这么干。

00:16:41.919 --> 00:16:50.320
I think all the variations, the idea that it was Yosemite Sandy was probably something we we came up with, but it was, we stole this bit from real life, like many an absurd bit.
我想所有的变化中，觉得那是优胜美地·桑迪的主意可能是我们自己想出来的，但这个桥段确实是我们从现实生活中偷来的，就像许多荒诞的桥段一样。

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:56.639
It's so fun to be a writer, because every little random dorm room memory is admissible.
当编剧太好玩了，因为每一个随意的宿舍记忆都是可以采纳的素材。

00:16:56.639 --> 00:16:57.480
Yeah.
对啊。

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:58.320
Like it actually helps your job.
就好像它真的对你的工作有帮助。

00:16:58.320 --> 00:16:59.159
Yeah, it's admissible in court.
对，在法庭上这也是可以采纳的。

00:16:59.159 --> 00:17:00.000
Yeah.
对。

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.759
It does, and you have, I think half the job being a writer, maybe 80%, is like 83%, is being a sponge, right?
确实如此，我认为作为一名编剧，一半的工作，也许是80%，大约83%，就是做一块海绵，对吧？

00:17:05.759 --> 00:17:09.559
It's like just sponging up details and going, this is gonna come out somehow.
就像是吸收各种细节，然后心想：这些总有一天会派上用场的。

00:17:09.559 --> 00:17:11.640
And having recall for them at the right moment.
并且在关键时刻能够回忆起它们。

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.039
Having recall, yeah, this fits.
能够回想起来，对，这很契合。

00:17:14.039 --> 00:17:14.831
this moment.
契合当前的时刻。

00:17:14.831 --> 00:17:16.149
That's Chris Harris.
这就是克里斯·哈里斯。

00:17:16.149 --> 00:17:17.336
Very good at that.
他在这方面非常擅长。

00:17:17.336 --> 00:17:18.918
So for a final question.
那么作为最后一个问题。

00:17:18.918 --> 00:17:23.532
Here's a another category of a big question here from Yasmine Zachary.
这里是来自雅斯明·扎卡里的另一个大问题。

00:17:23.532 --> 00:17:25.839
This episode plays with perception.
这一集探讨了感知。

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:32.555
Ted believes Mary is a prostitute, Robin pretends she doesn't care, and everyone's acting instead of just being honest.
泰德认为玛丽是个妓女，罗宾假装她不在乎，每个人都在演戏，而不是坦诚相待。

00:17:32.555 --> 00:17:34.192
So here's what I want to ask.
所以我想问的是：

00:17:34.192 --> 00:17:40.286
How much damage do we do to our relationships when we make assumptions instead of asking the hard questions?
当我们选择做出假设而不是去问那些艰难的问题时，我们会对彼此的关系造成多大的破坏？

00:17:40.286 --> 00:17:43.559
And why is pretending always easier than being vulnerable.
为什么假装总是比展现脆弱要容易？

00:17:43.559 --> 00:17:47.640
Wow, that's, uh, it's like smart question.
哇，这，呃，真是一个聪明的问题。

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:48.440
It's, it's great.
太棒了。

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.160
It's totally above our pay grade to answer this, but let's try.
回答这个问题完全超出了我们的能力范围，但我们可以试一试。

00:17:52.839 --> 00:17:56.119
That's like a great question to ask of like an actual expert.
这真是一个应该去问真正专家的好问题。

00:17:56.119 --> 00:17:58.200
My wife would be able to answer that question.
我妻子或许能回答这个问题。

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:01.960
Yeah, I was just going to say, it sounds like a question Jordana would ask.
对，我正想说，这听起来像是乔丹娜会问的问题。

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:09.319
I read a really interesting article on Substack that was like, don't let your life be guided by movie logic.
我最近在Substack上读到了一篇非常有趣的文章，大意是：不要让你的生活被电影逻辑所左右。

00:18:09.319 --> 00:18:17.579
And essentially what it was saying, like most plots in a movie are because people won't have hard conversations or uncomfortable conversations.
它本质上是在说，电影中的大多数情节之所以发生，是因为人们不愿进行艰难的对话或令人尴尬的对话。

00:18:17.579 --> 00:18:26.700
Like, you know, Goodwill Hunting could have stopped him and was like, you know, you seem like a really smart guy, but your anger is getting in the way of your moving forward.
比如，你知道的，《心灵捕手》里如果有人能拦住他说，你知道的，你看起来是个很聪明的人，但你的愤怒阻碍了你前进的脚步。

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:33.779
Like if someone had just like said these things, I mean, obviously you wouldn't have a movie to watch and that wouldn't be good.
如果有人直接对他说这些话，当然，那样你就没电影看了，那可不好。

00:18:33.779 --> 00:18:44.619
But the author was saying, don't let your own life be guided by what you see in movies, which is often talking around things, miscommunication.
但作者的意思是，不要让你的生活被你在电影中看到的东西所引导，电影中往往充斥着拐弯抹角和沟通不畅。

00:18:44.619 --> 00:18:50.539
And she did this example that I think she was in a law school or a graduate school.
她还举了一个例子，我想她当时在法学院或研究生院。

00:18:50.539 --> 00:19:01.339
And there was a woman, and I don't know if it was like a guy that they both liked or something where there was a miscommunication slash, they were at cross purposes for some reason.
有个女人，我不知道是不是她们俩都喜欢的某个男生，总之他们之间存在沟通不畅，或者说由于某种原因，他们的目的背道而驰。

00:19:01.339 --> 00:19:11.221
And she kind of glared at her, her friend, the woman across the room, and she marched right doctor already said, look, I think we got off on the wrong foot here and there might be a miscommunication.
她狠狠地瞪了她的朋友、房间那头的那个女人一眼，然后径直走过去说，听着，我觉得我们一开始就有些误会，可能存在沟通问题。

00:19:11.221 --> 00:19:13.058
I would really like to hash this out.
我真的很想把这件事彻底谈开。

00:19:13.058 --> 00:19:15.938
And the woman said, oh, my God, I'm so glad you said that.
那个女人说，哦，天哪，我太高兴你这么说了。

00:19:15.938 --> 00:19:17.577
I've been feeling the same thing.
我一直都有同感。

00:19:17.577 --> 00:19:20.506
She said she became my like ride or die best friend, right?
她说她成了我那种生死之交的最好朋友，对吧？

00:19:20.506 --> 00:19:28.451
And it's something Tim Ferris says that, that your success in life is directly proportional to the amount of uncomfortable conversations you're willing to have.
蒂姆·费里斯说过一句话，你在生活中的成功与你愿意进行的尴尬对话的数量成正比。

00:19:28.451 --> 00:19:32.970
And I just had two uncomfortable conversations with people that I needed to have yesterday.
我昨天刚和别人进行了两场我必须进行的尴尬对话。

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.809
And boy, last night, I couldn't have been in a better mood.
天哪，昨晚，我的心情简直好得不能再好了。

00:19:35.809 --> 00:19:36.809
I felt newly born.
我感觉自己获得了新生。

00:19:36.809 --> 00:19:43.609
It's what starts to cause death cookies, you know, that these things that are kind of on our list that are looming that you really don't want to do.
这些事情就是导致心理负担的元凶，你知道的，那些列在我们的待办清单上、挥之不去、你实在不想去面对的事情。

00:19:43.609 --> 00:19:47.049
But once you do them, it uncorks this enormous amount of life energy.
但一旦你完成了它们，就会释放出无比巨大的生命能量。

00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:59.930
When you say the thing you feel like socially, you're not supposed to say, or you know, but when you're vulnerable, I, you know, I'm a big fan of it, not maybe in the movie writing because we still want to have movies to watch where people don't know how to do this.
当你把那些按社会常规你觉得不该说的话说出口，或者你知道的，当你展现脆弱时，我，你知道的，我是它的超级粉丝，也许在写电影时不这样，因为我们依然想看那种人们不知该如何解决问题的电影。

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:06.029
But I think in life, it's a great idea to be as clear, you know, like Brene Brown says clear as kind.
但我认为在生活中，坦诚一点是个绝妙的主意，正如布琳·布朗所说：坦诚即善良。

00:20:06.029 --> 00:20:08.630
I have felt terrible since you told me yesterday.
自从你昨天告诉我之后，我一直觉得自己糟透了。

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:11.470
You never really liked me for 20 years, but I'm glad you slept great.
你二十年来其实从未真正喜欢过我，不过我很高兴你昨晚睡得很好。

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:13.390
Yeah, I'm so sorry, Craig.
对，真的很抱歉，克雷格。

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.710
Just for me, it was a great relief.
对我来说，这真是一种解脱。

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:22.630
I'm glad it's a load off.
我很高兴卸下了这个包袱。

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:23.630
It's so true.
确实如此。

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:30.230
And I think what I like about, again, I think things come out, I'm realizing like, I don't And this wasn't intentional.
而且我觉得我喜欢的是，我再次意识到有些事情会爆发，而且这不是刻意安排的。

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.470
This is the episode where we introduced group telepathy.
这就是我们引入集体心灵感应的那一集。

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:39.349
Maybe we introduced group telepathy because so much of the rest of the actual communication is bullshit in this episode.
也许我们引入集体心灵感应，是因为这一集中其余大部分的实际沟通简直全都是狗屁。

00:20:39.349 --> 00:20:42.109
They're all putting on some fucking bullshit in this episode.
他们在这一集里全都在满嘴胡说八道。

00:20:42.109 --> 00:20:43.789
This question is exactly right.
这个问题问得太对了。

00:20:43.789 --> 00:20:50.789
And at a certain moment in the episode, they have to resort to telepathy because no one's actually saying the things.
而且在这一集的某个时刻，他们不得不求助于心灵感应，因为实际上根本没人把话说出口。

00:20:50.789 --> 00:20:54.390
Some people are wearing masks they mean to be wearing.
有些人戴着他们故意要戴的面具。

00:20:54.390 --> 00:21:01.868
Mary has a mask thrust on her that's not her that's not the correct mask you know She's the only person telling the truth.
玛丽却被硬塞了一个不属于她的面具，那不是正确的面具，你知道的，她是唯一一个说实话的人。

00:21:01.868 --> 00:21:04.320
She's the only person telling the truth.
她是唯一一个说实话的人。

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.078
Yeah Well, that's it those are our questions.
对，好吧，就是这样，这些就是我们的问题。

